 q[4]['prompt']+=' Here ε is formal empty-string notation, not a literal engine character.'
 q[4]['workedExample']=q[4]['workedExample'].replace('so only rows with "no" for ε and "yes" for aaa match. A brute-force check over a⁰ to a⁸ confirms it.', 'and the three equivalent expressions each describe exactly a positive run of a. These two test strings alone would not prove equivalence for an arbitrary regex; checking a⁰ through a⁸ is additional finite evidence.')
 q[8]['options'][0]['explanation']='The four two-symbol prefixes are pairwise distinguishable by ε or a one-symbol suffix; two states cannot represent all four classes.'
 q[9]['options'][2]['text']='General backreferences, including (a+)b\\1'
 q[9]['options'][2]['explanation']='No. This particular pattern describes aⁿbaⁿ for n ≥ 1, a non-regular language. By contrast (a+)\\1 alone is regular, equivalent to (aa)+; the feature is rejected by these engines regardless.'
 q[9]['workedExample']=q[9]['workedExample'].replace('Backreferences are the line between "regex" and "regular". RE2, Go and Rust refuse them for exactly this reason.', 'General backreferences exceed finite-automaton expressiveness, though some specific patterns using them remain regular. RE2, Go regexp and Rust regex reject the unsupported syntax rather than analyze such special cases.')
